    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 BorderKeys contributors

    The keyboard module declares its own component. Nothing about this service lives in the
    application manifest, so the module can be built, tested and reasoned about on its own, and
    deleting the module deletes the component with it.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application>

        <!--
            The class name is fully qualified because this is a library: its manifest is merged
            into an application whose applicationId it does not share, so a leading-dot name
            would resolve against com.borderkeys rather than against this module.

            android:permission is what makes the service safe to export. Only the system holds
            BIND_INPUT_METHOD, so "exported" here means "bindable by the input method manager",
            not "bindable by any app that asks". Both attributes are required by the platform;
            an IME without them is never listed.

            directBootAware is false, and deliberately: everything this keyboard knows lives in
            a SQLCipher database whose key is in the Keystore, none of which is readable before
            the user has unlocked the device. A direct-boot-aware IME would have to run with no
            dictionary, no personal words and no clipboard, which is worse than the system
            falling back to its own keyboard on the lock screen.
        -->
        <service
            android:name="com.borderkeys.ime.BorderKeysService"
            android:label="@string/ime_name"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_INPUT_METHOD"
            android:exported="true"
            android:directBootAware="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.view.InputMethod" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.view.im"
                android:resource="@xml/method" />
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
